.TH ROTATE 1 "October 2026" "SASD Freeware" "User Commands"
.SH NAME
rotate \- retain ten numbered backup copies of a regular file
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B rotate
.I FILE
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B rotate
creates a copy of
.I FILE
in the same directory, keeping at most ten generations.
The source file is not changed.
.B FILE.0
is the newest snapshot;
.B FILE.9
is the oldest.
Before creating the next snapshot, the previous
.B FILE.9
is removed and lower generations are shifted upward.
Existing ordinary files or symbolic links with purely numeric suffixes
.B .10
and above are deleted.
Names such as
.B FILE.01
and
.B FILE.10.notes
are preserved.
.PP
The source must be an existing regular file, not a symbolic link.
Backup slots .0 through .9 must be ordinary files or absent.
A temporary
.B FILE.rotate.lock
directory blocks concurrent cooperating invocations.
.SH EXAMPLES
.TP
.B rotate ./hosts
Create ./hosts.0 while retaining ./hosts.
.TP
.B sudo rotate /etc/hosts
Rotate a protected system file (requires suitable directory permissions).
.TP
.B cp -p /etc/hosts.2 /etc/hosts
Manually restore an inspected generation (overwrites the original).
.SH EXIT STATUS
.TP
.B 0
Snapshot completed.
.TP
.B 2
Incorrect argument count.
.TP
.B other nonzero
Invalid file type, unavailable lock, insufficient permissions, or failed operation.
.SH NOTES
The operation is not transactionally atomic. If interrupted during
renames, inspect the backup generations before retrying. The script
snapshots a file but cannot guarantee a consistent copy while another
process writes it. The backup may contain confidential data. The copy
preserves metadata where supported and permitted. A stale lock may need
manual removal after ensuring no rotation is active.
.SH FILES
.TP
.I FILE.0 ... FILE.9
Numbered backup generations.
.TP
.I FILE.rotate.lock
Temporary concurrency lock directory.
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR cp (1),
.BR mv (1),
.BR logrotate (8)
